<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  vtmate in the background, started at login - a per-user launchd agent.

  A LaunchAgent (~/Library/LaunchAgents), not a LaunchDaemon (/Library/
  LaunchDaemons): a system daemon runs outside any user's session, with no
  $HOME, no display and no audio, so it could not do the global push-to-talk
  shortcut, the sounds, or anything this is actually for.

    install:  ./install-launchd.sh
              (or by hand: mkdir -p ~/Library/LaunchAgents
               cp com.vtmate.daemon.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
               launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.vtmate.daemon.plist)
    watch:    launchctl print gui/$(id -u)/com.vtmate.daemon
              tail -f ~/Library/Logs/vtmate.log
    remove:   ./uninstall-launchd.sh
    (older macOS: `launchctl load`/`unload` the plist path instead of
    bootstrap/bootout)

  This agent deliberately carries no configuration of its own. Set the
  daemon up once by hand with the flags you want.

  ProgramArguments runs vtmate through /bin/sh so it can be resolved at
  start rather than hardcoded: installer.sh's default (no scope/prefix
  flags given, i.e. the plain `curl ... | sh` from the README) installs to
  ~/.local/bin, which launchd's own minimal PATH for agents does not
  include - only a system scope install (/usr/local/bin) is on it.
  `command -v` checks PATH first (covering a system-wide install, or
  anywhere else you already added to it) and falls back to the installer's
  user-scope default. A custom install prefix matches neither: edit the
  command string below to that prefix's bin/vtmate path directly if that's
  what you used.

  vtmate needs the Accessibility permission (System Settings -> Privacy and
  Security -> Accessibility) to simulate the keystrokes behind reading a
  desktop selection and pasting dictated text. Grant it to the vtmate
  binary itself; since launchd starts it before you'd normally get prompted,
  you may need to run vtmate's daemon mode by hand once first so macOS
  asks, or add it manually in that Accessibility list (+ button, then
  locate the binary).

  Output is appended to ~/Library/Logs/vtmate.log by the shell command
  itself, not a plist-level StandardOutPath/StandardErrorPath: those are
  static strings launchd cannot expand "~" or "$HOME" in, so a fixed path
  like /tmp/vtmate.log would be shared (and its ownership fought over) by
  every user on a shared Mac who installs this same file.

  See ../../../README.md
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>Label</key>
	<string>com.vtmate.daemon</string>
	<key>ProgramArguments</key>
	<array>
		<string>/bin/sh</string>
		<string>-c</string>
		<string>mkdir -p "$HOME/Library/Logs" 2>/dev/null; exec "$(command -v vtmate 2>/dev/null || echo "$HOME/.local/bin/vtmate")" --daemon-foreground >>"$HOME/Library/Logs/vtmate.log" 2>&amp;1</string>
	</array>
	<key>RunAtLoad</key>
	<true/>
	<key>KeepAlive</key>
	<dict>
		<key>SuccessfulExit</key>
		<false/>
	</dict>
	<key>ProcessType</key>
	<string>Interactive</string>
</dict>
</plist>
